    "result_text": "The full test suite (the single `AcceptanceTest`) passes with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern exactly, keeping each concern in its proper layer:\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `isCityApproachingCapacity(String city)`, which returns true when the city's owner count is in `[40, 49]`. It reuses the existing `countByCityIgnoreCase` and the `CITY_CAPACITY` constant, with a new `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40`. The hard rejection (`isCityAtCapacity` at `>= 50`) is untouched.\n\n2. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (mapped to a new `capacity_warning` column) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 sets `owner.setCapacityWarning(...)` alongside the other computed flags, after the hard capacity rejection so the warning only applies to owners that are actually created.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it (MapStruct maps it by name automatically).\n\n5. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` (fill a city to 40, next owner warns) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` confirms the whole suite is green.",
